# LDAP Server Setup – Step by Step

## What is LDAP?

OpenLDAP (Lightweight Directory Access Protocol) is used to store and manage user information like usernames, passwords, groups, and permissions in a 
centralized directory server.

---

# Steps to Configure LDAP Server in Linux

## 1. Update the System

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y
```

---

## 2. Install OpenLDAP Server

For Ubuntu/Debian:

```bash
sudo apt install slapd ldap-utils -y
```

For RHEL/CentOS:

```bash
sudo yum install openldap openldap-servers openldap-clients -y
```

---

## 3. Configure LDAP Server

Reconfigure the package:

```bash
sudo dpkg-reconfigure slapd
```

During setup enter:

* DNS Domain Name → example.com
* Organization Name → ExampleOrg
* Administrator Password
* Database Backend → MDB
* Remove Database → No

---

## 4. Check LDAP Service Status

```bash
sudo systemctl status slapd
```

Start and enable service:

```bash
sudo systemctl enable slapd
sudo systemctl start slapd
```

---

# LDAP Important Terms

## DN (Distinguished Name)

Unique path of an LDAP entry.

Example:

```text
dn: uid=rahul,ou=users,dc=example,dc=com
```

---

## CN (Common Name)

Represents the name of object/user/group.

Example:

```text
cn: admin
```

---

## OU (Organizational Unit)

Used to organize users or groups.

Example:

```text
ou: users
```

---

## DC (Domain Component)

Represents domain name parts.

Example:

```text
dc=example,dc=com
```

---

# 5. Create Base Directory Structure

Create file:

```bash
nano base.ldif
```

Add:

```ldif
dn: ou=users,dc=example,dc=com
objectClass: organizationalUnit
ou: users

dn: ou=groups,dc=example,dc=com
objectClass: organizationalUnit
ou: groups
```

Apply configuration:

```bash
ldapadd -x -D "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com" -W -f base.ldif
```

---

# 6. Add New LDAP User

Create file:

```bash
nano user.ldif
```

Add:

```ldif
dn: uid=rahul,ou=users,dc=example,dc=com
objectClass: inetOrgPerson
objectClass: posixAccount
objectClass: shadowAccount
cn: Rahul Sharma
sn: Sharma
uid: rahul
uidNumber: 1001
gidNumber: 1001
homeDirectory: /home/rahul
loginShell: /bin/bash
userPassword: rahul123
```

Add user:

```bash
ldapadd -x -D "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com" -W -f user.ldif
```

---

# 7. Search LDAP Users

```bash
ldapsearch -x -LLL -b "dc=example,dc=com"
```

---

# 8. Create LDAP Group

Create file:

```bash
nano group.ldif
```

Add:

```ldif
dn: cn=developers,ou=groups,dc=example,dc=com
objectClass: posixGroup
cn: developers
gidNumber: 5000
```

Add group:

```bash
ldapadd -x -D "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com" -W -f group.ldif
```

---

# 9. Set LDAP User Password

```bash
ldappasswd -x -D "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com" -W \
-S "uid=rahul,ou=users,dc=example,dc=com"
```

---

# 10. Test LDAP Authentication

```bash
ldapwhoami -x -D "uid=rahul,ou=users,dc=example,dc=com" -W
```

---

# Common LDAP Commands

| Command    | Purpose         |
| ---------- | --------------- |
| ldapadd    | Add entries     |
| ldapsearch | Search entries  |
| ldapmodify | Modify entries  |
| ldapdelete | Delete entries  |
| ldappasswd | Change password |

---

# Important Object Classes

| ObjectClass        | Purpose          |
| ------------------ | ---------------- |
| inetOrgPerson      | User information |
| posixAccount       | Linux account    |
| organizationalUnit | Create OU        |
| posixGroup         | Linux group      |

